      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0021",
      "sequence": 876,
      "meaning": "Rāvaṇa orders the young monkey to guard his tongue, asks his name and why kinship should imply friendship. Aṅgada names himself as Bali’s son and asks whether they ever met. Rāvaṇa grows embarrassed and pretends merely to know of a monkey called Bali, then mocks Aṅgada as fire born to destroy his own clan and a useless ascetic’s messenger. Asked about Bali’s welfare, Aṅgada laughs: within ten days Rāvaṇa can embrace his friend Bali in the next world and learn what welfare comes from opposing Rāma. Real division enters only a heart without Raghubīra. Aṅgada concedes he destroys his clan while sarcastically calling Rāvaṇa its protector—though twenty eyes and ears should prevent such blindness and deafness.",
