    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0022",
      "sequence": 877,
      "meaning": "Aṅgada asks whether serving the one whose feet Śiva, Brahmā, gods and sages seek truly makes him a disgrace to his clan, and marvels that such thinking dwells in Rāvaṇa. Glaring, Rāvaṇa says he endures the monkey’s harshness because he knows policy and dharma. Aṅgada mocks that famous righteousness: stealing another’s wife, watching a messenger destroy the grove without drowning himself in shame, and forgiving the mutilation of his sister after seeing her noseless and earless. Rāvaṇa tells the fool to behold arms that eclipse the strength of world-guardians like Rāhu swallowing moons and that once lifted Kailāsa like a swan amid their lotus hands.",
