    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0023",
      "sequence": 878,
      "meaning": "Rāvaṇa asks what champion in Aṅgada’s host can face him. He belittles Rāma as weakened by separation, Lakṣmaṇa as grieving, Sugrīva and Vibhīṣaṇa as bank-side trees, Jāmbavān as old, and Nala and Nīla as mere craftsmen; only the monkey who burned Laṅkā seems strong. Aṅgada sarcastically agrees: such a small monkey burned only a small city, and Sugrīva had sent him merely for news; ashamed of disobeying orders, he has hidden and not returned. No one in their army is fit to fight Rāvaṇa, for policy matches love or hostility between equals; a lion gains no fame killing frogs. Though killing Rāvaṇa would diminish Rāma, warrior pride is hard to bear. Their barbed replies burn one another like arrows.",
