    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0024",
      "sequence": 879,
      "meaning": "Rāvaṇa praises a monkey’s single virtue: he dances shamelessly anywhere for his master and entertains people, expertly performing duty. He calls Aṅgada loyal and asks why he does not praise his lord. Claiming to appreciate merit, Rāvaṇa says he ignores the abuse. Aṅgada replies that Hanumān proved Rāvaṇa’s talent for appreciating merit: though he destroyed the grove, killed a son and burned the city, Rāvaṇa considered none of it an offence. Hence Aṅgada too became bold. When Rāvaṇa taunts that monkeys eat their fathers, Aṅgada says he would have eaten him after Bali, but spares a vessel of Bali’s pure fame. He recounts three Rāvaṇas: one tied by Bali’s children in a horse-stall, one captured by Sahasrabāhu and freed by Pulastya, and one kept in Bali’s armpit—then asks which is present.",
