      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0026",
      "sequence": 881,
      "meaning": "Angered, Aṅgada tells the arrogant wretch to measure his speech. Paraśurāma’s boundless forest of arms met an axe like consuming fire and his sharp weapon drowned countless kings, yet his pride fled at sight of the ‘man’ Rāma. Aṅgada asks how Rāma is merely human: by that logic Kāma is only an archer, Gaṅgā a river, Kāmadhenu an animal, the wish-tree a tree, nectar a drink, Garuḍa a bird, Śeṣa a snake, the wish-jewel a stone, Vaikuṇṭha a world and Raghupati’s unfailing devotion a mere gain. Hanumān churned Rāvaṇa’s pride, ruined his grove, burned his city and slew his son—how then did he leave as just a monkey?",
