    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0030",
      "sequence": 885,
      "meaning": "Aṅgada ends the verbal wrangle and orders Rāvaṇa to abandon pride. He did not come as a negotiator: Raghubīra sent him because killing a jackal brings no fame to an elephant. Remembering the Lord’s command, he has endured harsh words; otherwise he would smash Rāvaṇa’s faces and carry Sītā away by force. He knows the demon’s power from hearing how he abducted another’s wife from an empty hermitage. Only fear of insulting Rāma prevents him from dashing Rāvaṇa down, destroying his army and city, and carrying Jānakī away before all his women.",
